# Upstream source pin for metagoofil.  Shell-sourceable: debian/rules does
# ". debian/upstream.pin" and uses the variables directly.  Keep it to plain
# VAR=value assignments with no expansion, no quoting tricks and no commands.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# Every other kind:python tool in this repo installs from PyPI against a
# hash-pinned requirements.txt.  metagoofil cannot: upstream ships no setup.py
# and no pyproject.toml, so there is no sdist, no wheel, and no PyPI release to
# pin.  The source tree itself has to be fetched, and it therefore needs its
# own pinning mechanism with the same property -- network access may decide
# whether the build succeeds, never what lands in the package.
#
# WHY A COMMIT SHA AND NEVER A TAG OR BRANCH
#
# A git tag is a mutable pointer.  Anyone with push access can move v1.4.0 to
# different content, and a fetch by tag would silently pick it up.  A commit
# SHA is a content hash over the whole tree and its history: it cannot be moved
# to different content without breaking.  UPSTREAM_REF below is recorded for
# humans only -- nothing in the build reads it.
#
# WHY A SHA256 AS WELL
#
# The SHA is a hash of the git object, not of the tarball GitHub serves.  The
# sha256 pins the actual bytes we download, which is what sha256sum -c can
# check inside the build with no git available.
#
# ADJUDICATION PROCEDURE -- READ THIS BEFORE RE-PINNING
#
# GitHub's codeload tarballs are generated on demand, not stored.  Their
# checksums have changed historically when GitHub altered the compression
# envelope (gzip level, or git archive's internal format), with byte-identical
# file contents inside.  So a sha256 mismatch has two very different causes and
# they must be told apart.  If the build fails on the sha256sum check:
#
#   1. Do NOT simply re-pin the new sha256.  That is exactly the action an
#      attacker who can serve you a different tarball is relying on.
#
#   2. Extract the new tarball, and extract the pinned commit independently:
#
#        curl -fsSLo new.tar.gz "$UPSTREAM_URL"
#        mkdir new && tar -xzf new.tar.gz --strip-components=1 -C new
#        git clone https://github.com/opsdisk/metagoofil ref.git
#        git -C ref.git archive "$UPSTREAM_COMMIT" | (mkdir old && tar -x -C old)
#        diff -ru old new
#
#      Note that `git archive <commit>` is reproduced from the commit object
#      itself, so it is the authority here, not the tarball.
#
#   3. Identical tree  -> benign re-compression.  Re-pin UPSTREAM_SHA256, set
#      UPSTREAM_FETCHED to today, bump the package version by one +tlN, and say
#      in debian/changelog that the tree was verified unchanged against the
#      pinned commit.  UPSTREAM_COMMIT does not change.
#
#   4. Different tree  -> STOP.  The commit no longer resolves to the content
#      it did, which means a force-push, a repository takeover, or a tampered
#      download.  Do not re-pin, do not "just make the build green".  The build
#      failed closed, which is the designed behaviour.  Escalate.
#
# Bumping to a genuinely newer upstream release is a different operation: pick
# the new tag, resolve it to its commit with `git ls-remote --tags`, replace
# all of UPSTREAM_URL / UPSTREAM_SHA256 / UPSTREAM_COMMIT / UPSTREAM_REF /
# UPSTREAM_FETCHED together, and set the package version to the new upstream
# version.  Upstream's own requirements.txt must be re-read and the deps-only
# lock in ../requirements.txt regenerated at the same time.
#
# v1.4.0 is a lightweight tag: it points straight at the commit below, which is
# also the current head of master (resolved 2026-09-17 with
# `git ls-remote --tags https://github.com/opsdisk/metagoofil`).

UPSTREAM_URL=https://codeload.github.com/opsdisk/metagoofil/tar.gz/8d50624f1534e63c8bb64ee132d8c2298dba6724
UPSTREAM_SHA256=2e319f5396d9482cdbdac24b5e313fbc352239885455828019f3173fbeb81b32
UPSTREAM_COMMIT=8d50624f1534e63c8bb64ee132d8c2298dba6724
UPSTREAM_REF=v1.4.0
UPSTREAM_FETCHED=2026-09-17
